{% comment %}
  Star rating badge — the small "★★★★☆ (42)" line that sits under a product title.

  This block renders ENTIRELY from Shopify metafields, with no JavaScript and no network
  request. That is the whole point of writing reviews.rating and reviews.rating_count in
  src/lib/ratings.ts: the most-rendered widget on the most performance-sensitive page
  costs zero bytes of JS and zero round trips.

  Every competitor that fetches this over XHR pays a request, a layout shift, and a
  Lighthouse penalty on the exact page Shopify weights most heavily.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign rating = product.metafields.reviews.rating
  assign rating_count = product.metafields.reviews.rating_count.value | default: 0

  comment
    Where the count and the "be the first" link go.

    On the product's own page, a bare #reviewmaster-reviews: the review widget is on this
    page, the selected variant stays in the URL, and the widget script (which the review
    block loads) turns the jump into a smooth scroll that clears a sticky header, fetches
    the list if it has not been yet, or opens a floating or popup panel.

    Anywhere else (a featured product on the home page, a collection) a review widget on
    the page is not this product's, so the link goes to the product page and lands on its
    reviews there. A bare hash used to go nowhere at all on those pages.
  endcomment
  assign reviews_href = '#reviewmaster-reviews'
  if request.page_type != 'product' and product.url != blank
    assign reviews_href = product.url | append: '#reviewmaster-reviews'
  endif
-%}

{%- if rating != blank and rating_count > 0 -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    NOTE the property name. A `rating` metafield exposes a Liquid object whose numeric
    property is `.rating` — NOT `.value`. Writing `rating.value.value` resolves to nil,
    which `times: 1.0` turns into 0, so the widget renders "0.0" with five grey stars
    while the review COUNT beside it is correct. That mismatch is the tell.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign value = rating.value.rating | times: 1.0
    assign scale_max = rating.value.scale_max | times: 1.0
    if scale_max == 0
      assign scale_max = 5
    endif
    assign full_stars = value | floor
    assign remainder = value | minus: full_stars
    assign empty_stars = scale_max | minus: full_stars
    if remainder >= 0.25
      assign empty_stars = empty_stars | minus: 1
    endif
  -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    The average to one or two decimals.

    The metafield holds what src/lib/ratings.ts wrote, which is one decimal today ("4.9"),
    so two decimals shows "4.90" until the app writes more precision. One decimal is the
    default and is exactly the output every existing placement already had. Liquid's round
    is half-up, so a 4.95 stored with two decimals shows 5.0 at one, not 4.9. Liquid prints
    a whole or one-place number as "5.0" or "4.9" whatever it was rounded to, so for two
    decimals the second digit is padded here: "4.90", never a mix of 4.9 and 4.93.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign decimals = block.settings.value_decimals | default: '1' | plus: 0
    if decimals != 2
      assign decimals = 1
    endif
    assign shown_value = value | round: decimals | append: ''
    if decimals == 2
      assign value_parts = shown_value | split: '.'
      assign value_frac = value_parts[1] | default: ''
      if value_frac.size == 0
        assign shown_value = value_parts[0] | append: '.00'
      elsif value_frac.size == 1
        assign shown_value = shown_value | append: '0'
      endif
    endif
  -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Only emit the colour when the merchant actually changed it here.

    This block has no JavaScript and makes no request — that is the point of it, since it
    lands on collection pages where every byte is measured. So it cannot read the app's
    configured colour directly. Instead, leaving the variable unset lets it inherit the
    value the review widget publishes to the document root when it loads elsewhere on the
    page, and falls back to the CSS default otherwise.

    Emitting it unconditionally meant an untouched block always won, and the star colour in
    Settings -> Display could never reach this block at all.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign star_style = ''
    comment
      #e8871e is the default (Marka Orange); #f5a623 is the default before the rebrand. A block
      holding either was never changed, so the app's colour applies.
    endcomment
    if block.settings.star_color != blank and block.settings.star_color != '#e8871e' and block.settings.star_color != '#f5a623'
      assign star_style = '--rm-star-color:' | append: block.settings.star_color | append: ';'
    endif
  -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    The star shape. "app" (and a block saved before this setting existed) leaves it to
    Settings -> Display, which the review widget publishes on the page; a block on a page
    with no widget then shows the Marka tick-star, the default. Picking a shape here wins.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    data-rm-product lets the widget script tell a star block for another product (a
    featured product section on a product page) from this page's own, and send its link to
    that product's reviews instead of this one's.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div
    class="rm-stars rm-stars--{{ block.settings.size }}"
    style="{{ star_style }}"
    data-rm-product="{{ product.id }}"
    {%- if block.settings.star_shape == 'tick' or block.settings.star_shape == 'classic' %} data-rm-stars="{{ block.settings.star_shape }}"{% endif %}
    {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
  >
    {%- comment -%}
      role="img" with a single aria-label, rather than ten decorative star glyphs a screen
      reader would read out one at a time. The same rounded number as the visible one.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <span
      class="rm-stars__icons"
      role="img"
      aria-label="{{ 'reviewmaster.rating_aria' | t: value: shown_value, max: scale_max, count: rating_count }}"
    >
      {%- for i in (1..full_stars) -%}<span class="rm-star rm-star--full" aria-hidden="true">★</span>{%- endfor -%}
      {%- if remainder >= 0.25 -%}<span class="rm-star rm-star--half" aria-hidden="true">★</span>{%- endif -%}
      {%- for i in (1..empty_stars) -%}<span class="rm-star rm-star--empty" aria-hidden="true">★</span>{%- endfor -%}
    </span>

    {%- comment -%}
      The average as text, beside the stars and the count: the guidelines ask for rating,
      scale and count together, and it is the rating for anyone who cannot make out the
      stars. A block saved before this setting existed has no value, so blank counts as on.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if block.settings.show_value != false -%}
      <span class="rm-stars__value">{{ shown_value }}</span>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      "15 reviews" or "15 ratings": the merchant's word for it. A block saved before the
      setting existed has no value and keeps "reviews". Pluralised by the locale file
      (one/other), so a single review does not read "1 ratings". The "(15)" style keeps its
      look and gets the words as its accessible name, since "(15)" read out as a link says
      nothing about where it goes.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if block.settings.show_count -%}
      {%- liquid
        if block.settings.count_label == 'ratings'
          assign count_words = 'reviewmaster.rating_count' | t: count: rating_count
        else
          assign count_words = 'reviewmaster.review_count' | t: count: rating_count
        endif
      -%}
      <a
        class="rm-stars__count"
        href="{{ reviews_href }}"
        data-rm-product-url="{{ product.url }}"
        {%- if block.settings.count_style == 'parentheses' %} aria-label="{{ count_words }}"{% endif %}
      >
        {%- if block.settings.count_style == 'parentheses' -%}
          ({{ rating_count }})
        {%- else -%}
          {{ count_words }}
        {%- endif -%}
      </a>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

{%- elsif block.settings.show_when_empty -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    No reviews yet. Rendering a prompt keeps the vertical space stable so the page does not
    reflow once a first review lands, and gives the merchant a call to action.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="rm-stars rm-stars--empty-state" data-rm-product="{{ product.id }}" {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
    <a href="{{ reviews_href }}" data-rm-product-url="{{ product.url }}">{{ 'reviewmaster.be_first' | t }}</a>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Review stars",
  "target": "section",
  "stylesheet": "reviewmaster.css",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "size",
      "label": "Star size",
      "default": "medium",
      "options": [
        { "value": "small", "label": "Small" },
        { "value": "medium", "label": "Medium" },
        { "value": "large", "label": "Large" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "star_color",
      "label": "Star colour",
      "default": "#E8871E"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "star_shape",
      "label": "Star shape",
      "default": "app",
      "options": [
        { "value": "app", "label": "Same as the app" },
        { "value": "tick", "label": "Marka tick-star" },
        { "value": "classic", "label": "Classic star" }
      ],
      "info": "Same as the app follows Settings → Display in Marka Reviews."
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_value",
      "label": "Show the average rating",
      "default": true,
      "info": "The number beside the stars, such as 4.8."
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "value_decimals",
      "label": "Decimals in the average",
      "default": "1",
      "options": [
        { "value": "1", "label": "One (4.9)" },
        { "value": "2", "label": "Two (4.93)" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_count",
      "label": "Show review count",
      "default": true,
      "info": "The count links to the reviews further down the page."
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "count_style",
      "label": "Count style",
      "default": "parentheses",
      "options": [
        { "value": "parentheses", "label": "(42)" },
        { "value": "words", "label": "42 reviews (in words)" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "count_label",
      "label": "Count wording",
      "default": "reviews",
      "options": [
        { "value": "reviews", "label": "42 reviews" },
        { "value": "ratings", "label": "42 ratings" }
      ],
      "info": "Used when Count style is in words."
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_when_empty",
      "label": "Show a prompt when there are no reviews",
      "default": false,
      "info": "Only turn this on if the full review widget is NOT on this template — otherwise you get two 'write a review' prompts."
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
